Historical first MOV experiment (source at 4e3e3d1; qualification above applies):
2026-09-26 — K01b: userspace concurrent instruction-publication discrepancy.
This is diagnostic infrastructure compiled by the fixed GCC/Clang oracles,
NOT an EvoCC language-conformance test or an unchanged sched_ext acceptance.

One AArch64 MOV/RET function lives in a memfd. Three controls acknowledge each
generation before modification: RWX, RW/RX aliases, aliases plus reader ISB.
Five continuous controls patch the aligned MOV while readers call it; the
writer publishes data, enables code, disables code, obtains a writer-preferring
rwlock grace period, then retires data. Readers hold the matching read lock.
All use __builtin___clear_cache and registered PRIVATE_EXPEDITED_SYNC_CORE;
query/registration/syscall failures are never treated as successful execution.
Four pinned readers, 512 rounds, 20-second program bound; no RCU/static key,
BPF, CPU hotplug, kernel module, production patch, freed object or GDB.

Eight modes, same payload and Images in every flight:
                              GCC kernel         EvoCC kernel
MT TCG, Cortex-A72             3 PASS / 5 FAIL    3 PASS / 5 FAIL
HVF, host                     8 PASS / 0 FAIL    8 PASS / 0 FAIL
single-thread TCG, Cortex-A72  8 PASS / 0 FAIL    8 PASS / 0 FAIL
All six boots and orderly shutdowns PASS. All programs finish 512 rounds.
The sealed ARM64 container also passes all eight native program controls.

MT-TCG missing-publication counts (GCC kernel / EvoCC kernel):
GCC single mapping continuous       7871872 / 9283072
GCC aliases continuous              8882240 / 9003200
GCC aliases plus ISB                11780608 / 8373504
Clang single mapping continuous     6563072 / 10322368
Clang aliases continuous            8503040 / 8556800
Every initial bad-event snapshot reports returned 1 while the instruction
word reads 52800000 (mov w0, #0), inside the read-side critical section.
The three handshake controls each execute exactly 131072 calls without misses.
Every continuous control performs millions of calls; HVF/ST have zero misses.

This establishes a shared concurrent-code publication discrepancy outside
EvoCC-generated test code, kernel static keys and RCU. Aliasing alone, GCC-only
probe generation, and absence of a reader ISB do not explain it. It does NOT
yet identify the faulty emulator instruction/cache path or establish that a
specific fix resolves the original sched_ext NULL fault. Keep K01/K01b OPEN.
Do not silently switch the full runner to HVF (its hotplug path already fails),
relax its budget, suppress its assertions, or call an incomplete run PASS.

Build each with GCC 14.2.0-19 / Clang 19.1.7:
  COMPILER -static -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -pthread
    tests/fixtures/instruction-publish-alias.c -o smc-single
Executable-name symlinks select alias, continuous and isb; see main().
The fixed sealed image is 2c23684c9cc5ef5899073b59844fa47cc3c1d894f2316e21f25d5d85be5a10e2.
Native preparation uses four CPUs, 1 GiB, network none. Flights reuse the
64 MiB snapshot scratch disk; payload is 2380288 bytes with two ELF files and
symlink aliases, not eight binary copies. No full runtime disk or source clone.
ST's separate contract is diagnostic-only; locked acceptance files unchanged.
The prior signed-module disassembly also places __rcu_read_lock at offset a0
before its patchable branch at b0; it was not simply hoisted outside that call.

Linux contract: frozen include/uapi/linux/membarrier.h:89-116.
https://cdn.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/scheduler/membarrier.html
QEMU invalidation design (background, not proof of this specific bug):
https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/devel/multi-thread-tcg.html

